  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1119 PM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nTonight through Saturday...\n\nSoutherly winds remained breezy over northeast Nebraska this \nevening, with speeds of 12 to 20 mph, gusting up to 20 to 30 mph. A \nfew showers may sneak into far northeast Nebraska Saturday morning, \nahead of an approaching upper level shortwave and surface cold \nfront.  \n\nWe could still see a brief lull in precip during the late \nmorning/early afternoon hours, before additional showers develop \nbehind the passing cold front. Overall, rainfall is expected to \nremain confined to locations northwest of a line from Columbus, to \nWest Point, to Mapleton, IA. Temperatures will vary widely from \nnorthwest to southeast Saturday. Highs will range from the low 60s \nin the rain behind the front across northeast Nebraska, to the upper \n70s under sunnier skies in southeast Nebraska/southwest Iowa. \n\nSunday into Monday...\n\nRain chances continue for the northwestern half of the forecast area \nSunday and into Monday, as the remnants of an upper level trough \neject northeastward through the Central Plains. By Monday evening, \nlocations west of a line from Columbus to Sioux City have a 50-80% \nchance of seeing over an inch of rainfall. Meanwhile, Lincoln to \nOmaha, and areas to the southeast will be lucky to see 0.10\" (<25% \nchance). \n\nTemperatures continue to range widely from cloudy rain-soaked \nnortheast Nebraska in the 50s and low 60s, to mid and upper 70s \nunder southerly return flow in southeast NE/southwest IA. \n\nTuesday and Beyond...\n\nMost locations should see a break in rain chances during the day on \nTuesday, before the next trough moves from the intermountain west \ninto the Dakotas. Highs on Tuesday look to be a bit more seasonable \nfor the entire forecast area, with highs in the upper 50s and 60s. \n\nModel solutions continue to diverge as we head into midweek. Chances \nfor rain appear to be increasing for late Tuesday into Wednesday. \nHowever the forecast for Thursday and beyond is torn between the \nwetter GFS and notably drier ECMWF.",
